81 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 255 0 R
/E (The haenyeo are women free divers from Jeju Island who dive without breathing equipment. They harvest a wide range of marine life such as seaweed, abalone, and sea urchins. This practice has been developed and passed down over centuries. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
82 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 256 0 R
/E (The artwork is rooted in Hado-ri, a fishing village on Jeju Island where haenyeo culture has long shaped daily life. The village represents a community built on shared labour, collective organisation, and deep knowledge of the sea. Hado-ri reflects the social world from which the artwork emerges.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
83 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 257 0 R
/E (Seaweed harvested by the haenyeo has always been more than food. Seaweed has been traded for industrial use and also as a key ingredient in explosives. This demonstrates how natural resources can be repurposed depending on who controls them.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
84 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 258 0 R
/E (During the Japanese occupation, Hado-ri became an important site of anti-colonial resistance. Haenyeo and other villagers protested Japanese-controlled fishing cooperatives, unfair price controls, and discrimination that limited their freedom and economic rights.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
85 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 259 0 R
/E (In Seaweed Story, the performance of Jeju Arirang acts as a form of collective memory. It connects everyday marine labour to a history of resistance, resilience, and community solidarity.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
